[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3.4,2.5);
\draw (1.23,1.1) circle (0.8);
\draw (2.17,1.1) circle (0.8);
\node at (0.72,2.15) {$A$};
\node at (2.68,2.15) {$B$};
\node at (1.7,-0.35) {$\text{(i)}$};
\draw (4.2,0) rectangle (7.6,2.5);
\draw (5.43,1.1) circle (0.8);
\draw (6.37,1.1) circle (0.8);
\node at (4.92,2.15) {$A$};
\node at (6.88,2.15) {$B$};
\node at (5.9,-0.35) {$\text{(ii)}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
On diagram (i) shade the region $(A \cup B)'$.
On diagram (ii) shade the region $A' \cap B'$.
What do you notice?

mastery.solution_text:
On (i), $A \cup B$ is both whole circles, so its complement is everything outside them.
On (ii), $A'$ is everything outside circle $A$ and $B'$ is everything outside circle $B$, so their intersection is everything outside both.
[tikz]
